# Round 93: the round-91 slice hazard enumerated bound-vs-bound and OMITTED
# target-vs-bound, while the VM's OP_SLICE_GET pushes the TARGET first, then
# start, then end. A bound whose call rebinds the container had the AOT
# reading the NEW one: VM 3, AOT 18, both rc 0 -- and through norm, dot, the
# DEFAULT upper bound (which is the target's own length, so the length came
# from the post-call container too), and the bt fast path. Round 92's boxed
# read had turned a coin-flip into a deterministic target-last read.
#
# The target now hoists FIRST, and the incref is load-bearing rather than
# hygiene: the target read is a BORROW while the VM holds an owned ref on
# its stack across the bound evaluation, so without it a rebinding bound
# drops the last reference and the wrapper reads freed memory.
xs is [1, 2, 3, 4]
define shrink() as:
    xs is [9, 9]
    return 2
print of (sum of xs[0 : (shrink of [])])
print of xs
ys is [5, 6, 7, 8]
define grow() as:
    ys is [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]
    return 1
print of (sum of ys[(grow of []) :])
b is buffer of 4
b[0] is 1
b[1] is 2
b[2] is 3
b[3] is 4
define two() as:
    return 2
print of (sum of b[0 : (two of [])])
